#!/bin/bash

# Kali WSL Deploy Script (kali-deploy-wsl)
#
# Kali running as a WSL2 instance on a Windows host: the box you sit at all day
# and enter with `wsl.exe` from Windows Terminal.
#
# Why this is its own script rather than a flag on kali-deploy-remote:
# that script IS its access layer -- Tailscale SSH, a hardened OpenSSH, and a
# default-deny ufw. On WSL all three are wrong, so running it here meant running
# it with the whole thing switched off. A target that needs a script's defining
# feature disabled is a different target, not an option of that one, and SPEC.md
# splits on target for exactly this reason. So the access layer is absent here,
# not skipped: no tailscale, no sshd, no ufw, and no phases for them.
#
# What is left out, and why:
#   i3 / polybar / picom   no window manager. WSLg gives each GUI application
#                          its own Windows window, so there is no session for a
#                          WM to own and kali-desktop-i3 installs a stack that
#                          cannot start. GUI *applications* do work -- see the
#                          wslg phase, which makes them render on the GPU.
#   kitty                  the terminal is a Windows application. Windows
#                          Terminal draws this shell, so a Linux terminal
#                          emulator config would configure the wrong machine.
#   font probing           same reason: fc-list here answers a question about
#                          the guest, and the glyphs are rendered by the host.
#                          Install a Nerd Font on the WINDOWS side and point
#                          Windows Terminal at it; VINNY_ASCII=1 is the fallback.
#   tailscale / ssh / ufw  run Tailscale on the Windows host -- the tailnet is
#                          reachable from in here without a second tailscaled.
#                          WSL2 is NAT'd behind the host, so inbound is Windows
#                          Firewall's business, and a listening sshd on a box you
#                          enter with `wsl.exe` is a hole with no matching use.
#   hardware phases        no MAC cloning, no monitor mode, no bluetooth: the
#                          adapters belong to Windows and WSL2 sees none of them.
#                          The 802-11 / wireless / bluetooth / rfid metapackages
#                          are excluded from --with-metas for the same reason.
#
# What is added, that no other script has:
#   wslconf                /etc/wsl.conf -- systemd, interop, and the automount
#                          metadata option that makes permissions on /mnt/c sane
#                          instead of every file showing up 777 and root-owned.
#   wslg                   the GUI stack, when /mnt/wslg says there is a way to
#                          display a window: Mesa (so applications render on the
#                          GPU Windows already passes through as libd3d12.so
#                          rather than falling back to llvmpipe), Firefox, and
#                          the dconf system db Kali's profile promises but never
#                          builds. Not a desktop -- see above.
#   Windows interop        clip.exe-backed pbcopy/pbpaste, `winhome` for the
#                          host profile directory, and a tmux copy binding that
#                          puts the selection on the WINDOWS clipboard.
#   host-side browsing     the browser session stays on Windows. /usr/local/bin
#                          gets `edge` and `wsl-browser` as real commands, and
#                          $BROWSER points at the latter -- so gh, xdg-open and
#                          anything else that opens a page uses the host
#                          browser, not just the aliases you type yourself.
#                          Firefox is still installed, as the deliberate
#                          exception for traffic going through Burp or ZAP.
#
# Installs (native Kali apt only -- no /opt venv, no from-source builds):
#   nmap/ncat, binwalk, smbclient, hashcat, proxychains4, dns tools, nbtscan,
#   enum4linux(-ng), impacket-scripts, netexec, responder, mitm6, certipy-ad,
#   bloodhound.py, ffuf, gobuster, sqlmap, seclists, wordlists, java (headless),
#   tmux, zsh, and the tools the dots shell kit reaches for at runtime.
#
#   --with-metas additionally installs the software-only kali-tools-*
#   metapackages, for the breadth kali-deploy-physical gives a laptop. Off by
#   default because it is a multi-gigabyte install on a box whose disk is a
#   growable file on the host's NTFS volume.
#
# The dots shell kit (github.com/owlshells/dots) is deployed here exactly as it
# is by every other script in this repo -- see the dots phase.
#
# Usage:
#   sudo ./kali-deploy-wsl [options]
#
#   --dry-run          show what would happen; change nothing
#   --with-metas       also install the software-only kali-tools-* metapackages
#   --force            run even if this is not a WSL instance
#   --keep-shell       do not switch the login shell to zsh. A Kali WSL rootfs
#                      defaults to bash, and on a bash login ~/.zshrc is never
#                      sourced -- so by default the shell phase runs chsh, or
#                      everything it writes reaches nobody.
#   --skip <phase>     skip a phase (repeatable)
#   --only <phase>     run only these phases (repeatable)
#   --list-phases      print phase names and exit
#   --print-packages   print every package this script can install
#   --print-checked-binaries
#                      print every binary the verification pass ticks
#   -h, --help         this message
#
# Phases: base tools wslconf wslg tmux shell dots wordlists
#
# Environment knobs (all optional):
#   DOTS_TOKEN          GitHub token, only needed for a private fork. Without it
#                       the dots phase falls back to an SSH key, then `gh auth`,
#                       then a plain anonymous clone, then skips (the rest of
#                       the run is unaffected).
#   DOTS_REPO           owner/name of the shell kit (default owlshells/dots).
#   WSL_HOSTNAME        hostname to write into /etc/wsl.conf's [network] section.
#                       Unset leaves whatever WSL derives from the Windows host.
#
# Design notes: an installer should keep going when an optional piece fails
# rather than aborting. We keep -u and pipefail but NOT -e, and make every
# fallible step tolerant. Every phase is idempotent, so a run that dies partway
# can be resumed by running it again. A full transcript goes to /var/log.

set -uo pipefail

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Configuration
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

readonly WORDLIST_DIR="/usr/share/wordlists"
readonly ZSHRC_MARKER="# >>> kali-deploy-wsl >>>"
readonly ZSHRC_END_MARKER="# <<< kali-deploy-wsl <<<"
readonly WSLCONF="/etc/wsl.conf"

# Everything this script installs from Kali's repos. Kept as one array so
# --print-packages can hand it to the test harness and the two can never drift.
#
# Against kali-deploy-remote's list this drops openssh-server and ufw -- the
# access layer is not merely skipped here, it is not installed -- and folds in
# the web tooling that script holds back in a WSL-only array, since on this box
# it is not an addition but part of the baseline.
PKGS=(
    curl
    wget
    git
    jq
    unzip
    ca-certificates
    gnupg
    python3
    python3-pip
    pipx

    zsh
    tmux
    iproute2

    nmap
    ncat
    binwalk
    smbclient
    hashcat
    proxychains4
    # dnsutils was renamed bind9-dnsutils; the old name has no candidate on
    # current Kali, so naming it quietly installed nothing and left the box
    # without dig or nslookup.
    bind9-dnsutils
    dnsrecon
    dnsenum
    nbtscan
    enum4linux
    enum4linux-ng

    python3-impacket
    impacket-scripts
    netexec
    responder
    mitm6
    certipy-ad
    bloodhound.py

    # Web tooling. kali-deploy-remote keeps these in a WSL-only array because
    # its own target is a box you drive from elsewhere; this box is the
    # workstation, so a fuzzer and an injection tool are baseline, not an extra.
    ffuf
    gobuster
    sqlmap

    seclists
    wordlists

    # Command library / cheat-sheets. Same {{placeholder}} grammar as the
    # snippets that used to live in the dots repo, 200+ sheets, and Kali
    # maintains it so updates arrive via apt.
    arsenal-ng

    # Terminal file manager, for the loot/output tree an engagement leaves
    # behind. The shell phase adds the `lfcd` wrapper that makes quitting lf
    # leave you in the directory you ended up in.
    lf

    # What the dots shell kit reaches for at runtime.
    #
    # dots writes the integrations for these and installs none of them -- its
    # tools/install-tools.sh is opt-in and the dots phase deliberately does not
    # run it, because that script also does a pipx install and three `go install`
    # builds, which SPEC.md rules out. So they arrive from the distro.
    #
    # Without them the box deploys clean and the shell is half-wired: ^X^R recall
    # answers "fzf not installed", `payload` the same, `denv` cannot auto-load an
    # .envrc, `ll` falls back to plain ls, and `listen` drops to bare nc.
    #
    # Debian ships bat's binary as batcat; dots' aliases already handle both.
    fzf         # ^X^R recall, payload, and the Ctrl-R/Ctrl-T/Alt-C bindings
    bat         # preview pane in payload and the recall picker
    eza         # the ll alias
    direnv      # denv -- per-engagement .envrc auto-load
    rlwrap      # listen's readline upgrade, ahead of bare nc
)

# On wslu, and why there is no WSL-specific package array:
#
# The obvious candidate was wslu, which provides wslview (open a URL or file
# with the Windows default handler). Kali does not carry it -- no candidate in
# kali-last-snapshot main, contrib or non-free -- so listing it would have meant
# a name that never installs sitting in --print-packages, which is precisely
# what T1 exists to reject. Adding the Debian repo that does carry it is ruled
# out by SPEC.md's rule against mixing archives into a rolling release.
#
# So nothing is installed for interop, and none is needed: clip.exe,
# powershell.exe, cmd.exe and explorer.exe come from the Windows host through
# [interop], and the shell phase probes for wslview at runtime, using it if you
# have installed it by hand and falling back to explorer.exe otherwise. The
# verification pass reports wslview with check_opt for the same reason -- an X
# that is red on every correct run trains you to skim past the ones that matter.

# Software-only Kali metapackages, for --with-metas.
#
# This is kali-deploy-physical's TOOL_METAS with the hardware categories
# removed: 802-11, wireless, bluetooth and rfid all drive adapters that belong
# to the Windows host. WSL2 has no USB passthrough without usbipd and no raw
# monitor-mode capability at all, so those four install a large dependency tree
# for tools that cannot work here.
#
# sniffing-spoofing stays: tcpdump and friends are perfectly usable against the
# instance's own NAT'd interface even though a wireless capture is not.
TOOL_METAS=(
    kali-tools-information-gathering
    kali-tools-vulnerability
    kali-tools-web
    kali-tools-database
    kali-tools-passwords
    kali-tools-exploitation
    kali-tools-post-exploitation
    kali-tools-sniffing-spoofing
    kali-tools-windows-resources
    kali-tools-reverse-engineering
)

# The GUI stack, installed only when WSLg is actually present: Mesa's userspace
# plus a browser to have something to point it at.
#
# Windows passes the GPU into the instance as /usr/lib/wsl/lib/libd3d12.so (plus
# libd3d12core and libdxcore). Nothing in Kali uses it on its own: the bridge is
# Mesa's d3d12 gallium driver, which lives in libgl1-mesa-dri. Without these, a
# GUI app still runs under WSLg but falls back to software rendering, and
# Firefox says so in the way that sends you looking for a broken display:
#
#   [GFX1-]: glxtest: libpci missing
#   [GFX1-]: glxtest: libEGL missing
#   [GFX1-]: glxtest: EGL test failed
#   [GFX1-]: No GPUs detected via PCI
#
# Every one of those four lines is a missing package from this list, not a
# missing GPU. libpci3 is what lets glxtest enumerate the PCI bus at all, which
# is why its absence reads as "No GPUs detected".
#
# Gated on /mnt/wslg rather than installed unconditionally: on WSL1, or on a
# Windows build without WSLg, there is no way to display a window and this is
# ~200MB of driver for nothing. Probing the capability rather than inferring it
# from a version is the same rule SPEC.md sets for distro version checks.
#
# mesa-va-drivers is deliberately absent -- it has no candidate on Kali, and
# hardware video decode is not what any of this box's tooling needs.
WSLG_PKGS=(
    libgl1
    libegl1
    libglx-mesa0
    libgl1-mesa-dri
    libpci3
    mesa-utils      # glxinfo, so the verification pass can prove the d3d12
                    # driver is actually the one in use rather than assuming it

    # The `dconf` command itself. dconf-gsettings-backend is already pulled in
    # by the GTK stack and is NOT this -- it provides the backend library, not
    # the binary, so the db-compiling step below found no `dconf` on the first
    # real run and skipped itself. Another instance of naming the obvious
    # package and getting a silent no-op.
    dconf-cli

    # Named rather than assumed. It is on a stock Kali image, so on this box it
    # looked present whether or not anything installed it -- which is the ISO's
    # doing and evaporates on a netinst, exactly the case SPEC.md says to name.
    #
    # It is firefox-esr, not firefox: plain `firefox` has NO candidate on Kali,
    # so naming the obvious one installs nothing and says nothing, the same
    # shape of bug as dnsutils -> bind9-dnsutils. The package then diverts
    # /usr/bin/firefox, so the BINARY to check for is `firefox` while the
    # PACKAGE is firefox-esr -- the certipy-ad mismatch again, in reverse.
    #
    # A browser earns its place on a box with a display: Burp and ZAP need
    # something to proxy, and an HTML report needs something to open it.
    firefox-esr
)

readonly ALL_PHASES=(base tools wslconf wslg tmux shell dots wordlists)

# Binaries the verification pass ticks at the end of a run. BINARY names, not
# package names -- certipy-ad ships /usr/bin/certipy-ad, while /usr/bin/certipy
# belongs to python3-certipy, an unrelated cert library.
#
# A tick has to mean something, so a binary whose name differs from its package
# is left out rather than guessed at: `bat` installs as batcat on Debian and
# `arsenal-ng`'s entry point is not `arsenal-ng`, so neither is checked here.
VERIFY_BINS_TOOLS=(
    nmap ncat binwalk smbclient hashcat proxychains4 dig enum4linux
    enum4linux-ng netexec responder mitm6 certipy-ad bloodhound-python
    impacket-ntlmrelayx ffuf gobuster sqlmap
)
VERIFY_BINS_SHELL=(
    tmux zsh git lf fzf eza direnv rlwrap
)

# Colors
readonly RED='\033[0;31m'
readonly GREEN='\033[0;32m'
readonly YELLOW='\033[1;33m'
readonly BLUE='\033[0;34m'
readonly NC='\033[0m'

DRY_RUN=0
WITH_METAS=0
FORCE=0
KEEP_SHELL=0
SKIP_PHASES=()
ONLY_PHASES=()

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Helper Functions
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# warn/err go to stderr so they don't get captured by $(...) command
# substitutions (e.g. get_real_user) and so they're visible on the error path.
msg()   { echo -e "${GREEN}[+]${NC} $1"; }
warn()  { echo -e "${YELLOW}[*]${NC} $1" >&2; }
err()   { echo -e "${RED}[!]${NC} $1" >&2; }
phase() { echo -e "\n${BLUE}=== $1 ===${NC}"; }

# Help text is the header comment block itself. Read it structurally -- from
# line 3 to the first line that isn't a comment -- rather than by hardcoded line
# numbers, so editing the header can't leak code into --help.
usage() {
    awk 'NR>2 { if (/^#/) { sub(/^# ?/, ""); print } else { exit } }' "$0"
    exit 0
}

get_real_user() {
    if [[ -n "${SUDO_USER:-}" ]] && [[ "$SUDO_USER" != "root" ]]; then
        echo "$SUDO_USER"
    else
        err "This script must be run with sudo, not as root directly"
        err "Usage: sudo $0"
        exit 1
    fi
}

get_user_home() {
    getent passwd "$1" | cut -d: -f6
}

# Every mutating command goes through run() so --dry-run is honoured in one
# place rather than being sprinkled through each phase.
run() {
    if (( DRY_RUN )); then
        echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} $*"
        return 0
    fi
    "$@"
}

# Write a heredoc to a file, honouring --dry-run. Reads content from stdin.
write_file() {
    local dst="$1" mode="${2:-}"
    if (( DRY_RUN )); then
        echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} write $dst"
        cat >/dev/null
        return 0
    fi
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$dst")"
    cat > "$dst" || { err "could not write $dst"; return 1; }
    [[ -n "$mode" ]] && chmod "$mode" "$dst"
    return 0
}

# Copy a file aside once, the first time we are about to replace it. Copying
# unconditionally would mean the second run overwrites the backup with our own
# generated content, leaving the original recoverable exactly once.
backup_once() {
    local f="$1"
    [[ -f "$f" && ! -f "$f.backup" ]] || return 0
    run cp "$f" "$f.backup" && msg "backed up $(basename "$f") to $(basename "$f").backup"
}

should_run() {
    local p="$1" x
    if (( ${#ONLY_PHASES[@]} )); then
        for x in "${ONLY_PHASES[@]}"; do [[ "$x" == "$p" ]] && return 0; done
        return 1
    fi
    for x in "${SKIP_PHASES[@]}"; do [[ "$x" == "$p" ]] && return 1; done
    return 0
}

is_phase() {
    local p="$1" x
    for x in "${ALL_PHASES[@]}"; do [[ "$x" == "$p" ]] && return 0; done
    return 1
}

# True inside a WSL instance.
#
# Lifted verbatim from kali-deploy-remote, including the reasoning, because the
# subtlety it encodes is the same one here and a second, looser copy would be
# the bug. Keyed on userspace artifacts, never on the kernel name alone: a
# container shares its host's kernel, so on a WSL2 host
# /proc/sys/kernel/osrelease reads "...-microsoft-standard-WSL2" inside every
# container too -- including the one this repo's own test suite runs in. A bare
# `grep microsoft` there would let this script's WSL gate pass inside a test
# container, and would do it only on a WSL developer's machine, never in CI.
#
# /run/WSL and /mnt/wsl are created by the WSL2 init inside the instance and are
# absent in a container on the same host. WSL1 has neither, so it falls back to
# the WSL /init plus the kernel string -- a pair a container cannot produce,
# since it has no /init.
#
# $WSL_DISTRO_NAME is deliberately unused: sudo scrubs it from the environment,
# so it is empty exactly when this script runs.
is_wsl() {
    [[ -d /run/WSL || -d /mnt/wsl ]] && return 0
    [[ -e /init ]] && grep -qi microsoft /proc/sys/kernel/osrelease 2>/dev/null
}

# True if systemd is PID 1. WSL only does this with `systemd=true` under [boot]
# in /etc/wsl.conf, and without it `systemctl` is not merely unavailable -- it
# exits nonzero in a way that makes any service step a silent no-op.
has_systemd() {
    [[ "$(ps -p 1 -o comm= 2>/dev/null)" == "systemd" ]]
}

# True if apt has an installation candidate for the package on THIS system.
# Lets us skip packages that were renamed/removed instead of aborting the batch.
apt_available() {
    local cand
    cand=$(apt-cache policy "$1" 2>/dev/null | awk '/Candidate:/ {print $2}')
    [[ -n "$cand" && "$cand" != "(none)" ]]
}

# Install a list of apt packages, falling back to one-by-one so that a single
# unavailable package doesn't take down the entire batch. In the fallback we
# probe availability first so a removed/renamed package is a clean skip.
apt_install() {
    (( $# )) || return 0
    if (( DRY_RUN )); then
        echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} apt-get install -y $*"
        return 0
    fi
    if apt-get install -y "$@"; then
        return 0
    fi
    warn "Bulk apt install failed; retrying packages individually..."
    for pkg in "$@"; do
        if apt_available "$pkg"; then
            apt-get install -y "$pkg" || warn "Could not install '$pkg' (skipping)"
        else
            warn "Package '$pkg' not available on this system (skipping)"
        fi
    done
}

# Install the FIRST package from the list that this system actually offers.
apt_install_first() {
    local label="$1"; shift
    local pkg
    for pkg in "$@"; do
        if apt_available "$pkg"; then
            if (( DRY_RUN )); then
                echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} $label: would install '$pkg'"
                return 0
            fi
            if apt-get install -y "$pkg"; then
                msg "$label: installed '$pkg'"
                return 0
            fi
        fi
    done
    warn "$label: none of [$*] are available (skipping)"
    return 1
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Argument parsing
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
        --dry-run)      DRY_RUN=1; shift ;;
        --with-metas)   WITH_METAS=1; shift ;;
        --force)        FORCE=1; shift ;;
        --keep-shell)   KEEP_SHELL=1; shift ;;
        --skip)         [[ -n "${2:-}" ]] || { err "--skip needs a phase"; exit 1; }
                        SKIP_PHASES+=("$2"); shift 2 ;;
        --only)         [[ -n "${2:-}" ]] || { err "--only needs a phase"; exit 1; }
                        ONLY_PHASES+=("$2"); shift 2 ;;
        --list-phases)  printf '%s\n' "${ALL_PHASES[@]}"; exit 0 ;;
        # Every package name this script can install, one per line. The test
        # harness reads its package list from here so it can never drift out of
        # sync with the arrays above. The metapackages are printed too: the help
        # says every package the script CAN install, and a set that only appeared
        # under --with-metas would be the one part no test ever looked at.
        --print-packages)
                        printf '%s\n' "${PKGS[@]}" "${WSLG_PKGS[@]}" \
                                      "${TOOL_METAS[@]}"; exit 0 ;;
        # Every binary the verification pass ticks. Cross-checked against the
        # install closure of --print-packages by the test suite.
        --print-checked-binaries)
                        printf '%s\n' "${VERIFY_BINS_TOOLS[@]}" \
                                      "${VERIFY_BINS_SHELL[@]}"; exit 0 ;;
        -h|--help)      usage ;;
        *)              err "unknown option: $1"; err "try --help"; exit 1 ;;
    esac
done

for p in "${SKIP_PHASES[@]}" "${ONLY_PHASES[@]}"; do
    is_phase "$p" || { err "unknown phase: $p"; err "valid: ${ALL_PHASES[*]}"; exit 1; }
done

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pre-flight
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [[ $EUID -ne 0 ]]; then
    err "This script must be run with sudo"
    exit 1
fi

# get_real_user's `exit 1` only exits the command-substitution subshell, so we
# must check its status and exit the parent ourselves (no `set -e` to do it).
REAL_USER=$(get_real_user) || exit 1
USER_HOME=$(get_user_home "$REAL_USER")

if [[ -z "$REAL_USER" || -z "$USER_HOME" ]]; then
    err "Could not determine the target user or home directory"
    exit 1
fi

# The WSL gate is a hard stop, the inverse of kali-deploy-remote's soft detect.
# That script adapts to WSL because it also has a non-WSL target; this one has
# nothing else to be. Running it on a physical box would write a wsl.conf that
# nothing reads and a shell block full of interop aliases pointing at .exe files
# that are not there -- a working machine, quietly configured for the wrong one.
if ! is_wsl; then
    if (( FORCE )); then
        warn "This is not a WSL instance, but --force was given -- continuing."
        warn "  The wslconf phase and the Windows interop aliases will be useless here."
    else
        err "This is not a WSL instance."
        err "  For a physical laptop use kali-deploy-physical."
        err "  For a headless box or teamserver use kali-deploy-remote."
        err "  To run this anyway: sudo $0 --force"
        exit 1
    fi
fi

LOGFILE="/var/log/kali-deploy-wsl-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).log"
if (( ! DRY_RUN )); then
    exec > >(tee -a "$LOGFILE") 2>&1
fi

phase "Pre-flight"
msg "User:      $REAL_USER ($USER_HOME)"
(( DRY_RUN )) && msg "Mode:      DRY RUN (nothing will be changed)" \
              || msg "Log:       $LOGFILE"

# Advisory, not fatal. /etc/os-release on kali-rolling has ID=kali but NO
# VERSION_ID, so never branch on version: under `set -u` that is a crash.
distro_id="$(. /etc/os-release 2>/dev/null && echo "${ID:-unknown}")"
if [[ "$distro_id" != "kali" ]]; then
    warn "This does not look like Kali (ID=$distro_id)."
    warn "Continuing anyway -- every package is probed before install."
else
    msg "Distro:    Kali"
fi

msg "Target:    WSL ($(cat /proc/sys/kernel/osrelease 2>/dev/null))"

if has_systemd; then
    msg "Init:      systemd (PID 1)"
else
    warn "Init:      not systemd -- add 'systemd=true' under [boot] in $WSLCONF"
    warn "           the wslconf phase will offer to do this; it needs a"
    warn "           'wsl --shutdown' from Windows before it takes effect"
fi

# /mnt/wslg is created by the WSLg init. Reported, never acted on: WSLg means
# GUI applications work, it does not mean a window manager has a session to own,
# which is why no part of this script installs one.
if [[ -d /mnt/wslg ]]; then
    msg "WSLg:      present (the wslg phase installs the GUI stack; no WM -- see header)"
else
    msg "WSLg:      absent (CLI only; the wslg phase will skip itself)"
fi

(( WITH_METAS )) && msg "Metas:     --with-metas (${#TOOL_METAS[@]} kali-tools-* metapackages)"
(( ${#SKIP_PHASES[@]} )) && msg "Skipping:  ${SKIP_PHASES[*]}"
(( ${#ONLY_PHASES[@]} )) && msg "Only:      ${ONLY_PHASES[*]}"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase: base -- apt update / full-upgrade, debconf pre-seed
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if should_run base; then
    phase "Phase: base"

    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

    msg "Updating package lists..."
    run apt-get update || warn "apt-get update reported errors (continuing)"

    # Kali is a rolling release: `upgrade` can hold back packages and leave a
    # partially-upgraded system. full-upgrade is the supported path.
    #
    # confdef/confold keep existing config files without asking, so a conffile
    # prompt cannot hang a run that is meant to be unattended.
    msg "Full-upgrading (rolling release; 'upgrade' is not safe here)..."
    run apt-get -y \
        -o Dpkg::Options::=--force-confdef \
        -o Dpkg::Options::=--force-confold \
        full-upgrade || warn "full-upgrade reported errors (continuing)"

    # Pre-seed the Kali packages that ask debconf questions. They arrive as
    # dependencies as the tool list grows, and an unattended run has nobody to
    # answer a prompt -- it just hangs. The safe answer to all of them is "no".
    msg "Pre-seeding debconf (prevents interactive hangs mid-install)..."
    if (( DRY_RUN )); then
        echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} debconf-set-selections <<< (wireshark/kismet/macchanger)"
    else
        debconf-set-selections <<'PRESEED' || warn "debconf pre-seed failed"
wireshark-common wireshark-common/install-setuid boolean false
kismet kismet/install-setuid boolean false
kismet kismet/install-users string
macchanger macchanger/automatically_run boolean false
PRESEED
    fi
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase: tools
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if should_run tools; then
    phase "Phase: tools"

    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

    install_pkgs=("${PKGS[@]}")

    msg "Installing ${#install_pkgs[@]} packages from the Kali repos..."
    apt_install "${install_pkgs[@]}"

    if (( WITH_METAS )); then
        # One at a time, not one bulk call. A metapackage whose dependency tree
        # is briefly unresolvable during a rolling-release churn window takes
        # only itself down this way -- and these are large enough that losing
        # the batch means losing a lot of already-downloaded work.
        msg "Installing ${#TOOL_METAS[@]} kali-tools-* metapackages (this is large)..."
        for meta in "${TOOL_METAS[@]}"; do
            if apt_available "$meta"; then
                apt_install "$meta"
            else
                warn "metapackage '$meta' has no candidate here (skipping)"
            fi
        done
    fi

    # No GUI: default-jre-headless tracks the current default JDK, and the
    # explicit names are only fallbacks. Headless even when WSLg is present --
    # the AWT stack a full JRE pulls in is not what bloodhound.py or a JAR-based
    # tool needs from a shell.
    apt_install_first "Java runtime (headless)" \
        default-jre-headless \
        openjdk-21-jre-headless \
        openjdk-17-jre-headless
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase: wslconf -- /etc/wsl.conf
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The one phase with no counterpart in any other script, because it configures
# the boundary with the Windows host rather than anything inside Kali.
#
# Non-destructive by construction. wsl.conf is frequently hand-edited -- it is
# how you set systemd, the default user, and mount behaviour -- so this phase
# will create the file, and will append a section that is entirely absent, but
# never rewrites a section that is already there. A file whose [boot] says
# something different from what we would write is a decision someone made, and
# silently reversing it is exactly the kind of surprise a deploy script must not
# spring on a box you already work on. Where a section exists but lacks a key we
# want, the phase prints the line to add instead of editing around it.

if should_run wslconf; then
    phase "Phase: wslconf"

    # Read back which [sections] the current file defines. Leading whitespace is
    # tolerated because hand-edited INI usually has some.
    wslconf_has_section() {
        [[ -f "$WSLCONF" ]] || return 1
        grep -qE "^[[:space:]]*\[$1\][[:space:]]*$" "$WSLCONF"
    }

    wslconf_has_key() {
        [[ -f "$WSLCONF" ]] || return 1
        grep -qE "^[[:space:]]*$1[[:space:]]*=" "$WSLCONF"
    }

    if [[ ! -f "$WSLCONF" ]]; then
        msg "no $WSLCONF -- writing one"
        write_file "$WSLCONF" 644 <<WSLCONF_NEW
# /etc/wsl.conf -- written by kali-deploy-wsl
# Takes effect after 'wsl --shutdown' from Windows, not on the next shell.

[boot]
# systemd as PID 1. Without it systemctl is not just unavailable, it fails in a
# way that makes every service step a silent no-op.
systemd=true

[automount]
# metadata is the one that matters: without it every file under /mnt/c reads as
# 0777 root:root, so chmod is a no-op, ssh refuses to use a key stored there,
# and git reports the whole tree as mode-changed.
options="metadata,umask=22,fmask=11"

[interop]
# Keep .exe interop on: clip.exe, explorer.exe and wslview all go through it,
# and the shell phase below wires up aliases that depend on it.
enabled=true
appendWindowsPath=true
WSLCONF_NEW
        msg "wrote $WSLCONF (boot/automount/interop)"
        WSLCONF_CHANGED=1
    else
        msg "$WSLCONF exists -- adding only what is absent, editing nothing"
        WSLCONF_CHANGED=0

        if wslconf_has_section boot; then
            if wslconf_has_key systemd; then
                msg "  [boot] systemd already set -- leaving it"
            else
                warn "  [boot] exists but has no systemd key. To enable it, add:"
                warn "      systemd=true"
            fi
        else
            backup_once "$WSLCONF"
            if (( DRY_RUN )); then
                echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} append [boot] to $WSLCONF"
            else
                printf '\n[boot]\nsystemd=true\n' >> "$WSLCONF"
            fi
            msg "  added [boot] systemd=true"
            WSLCONF_CHANGED=1
        fi

        if wslconf_has_section automount; then
            wslconf_has_key options \
                && msg "  [automount] options already set -- leaving it" \
                || warn "  [automount] exists without options; consider: options=\"metadata,umask=22,fmask=11\""
        else
            backup_once "$WSLCONF"
            if (( DRY_RUN )); then
                echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} append [automount] to $WSLCONF"
            else
                printf '\n[automount]\noptions="metadata,umask=22,fmask=11"\n' >> "$WSLCONF"
            fi
            msg "  added [automount] options (metadata -- makes chmod work under /mnt/c)"
            WSLCONF_CHANGED=1
        fi

        if wslconf_has_section interop; then
            msg "  [interop] already present -- leaving it"
        else
            backup_once "$WSLCONF"
            if (( DRY_RUN )); then
                echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} append [interop] to $WSLCONF"
            else
                printf '\n[interop]\nenabled=true\nappendWindowsPath=true\n' >> "$WSLCONF"
            fi
            msg "  added [interop] (clip.exe / wslview depend on it)"
            WSLCONF_CHANGED=1
        fi
    fi

    # Only on request: WSL derives a hostname from the Windows machine name, and
    # overriding it is a preference, not a fix.
    if [[ -n "${WSL_HOSTNAME:-}" ]]; then
        if wslconf_has_section network; then
            msg "[network] already present -- not touching it (WSL_HOSTNAME ignored)"
        else
            backup_once "$WSLCONF"
            if (( DRY_RUN )); then
                echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} append [network] hostname=$WSL_HOSTNAME"
            else
                printf '\n[network]\nhostname=%s\ngenerateHosts=true\n' "$WSL_HOSTNAME" >> "$WSLCONF"
            fi
            msg "set hostname to $WSL_HOSTNAME"
            WSLCONF_CHANGED=1
        fi
    fi

    if (( WSLCONF_CHANGED )) && ! has_systemd; then
        warn "$WSLCONF changed and systemd is not PID 1 yet."
        warn "  From a Windows terminal, run:  wsl --shutdown"
        warn "  then start Kali again. Nothing here needs re-running afterwards."
    fi
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase: wslg -- make GUI applications render on the GPU instead of the CPU
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This is the one place the script touches graphics, and it stops well short of
# a desktop. No window manager, no compositor, no display manager: WSLg gives
# each GUI application its own Windows window, so there is no session for a WM
# to own and installing one gets you a stack that cannot start. What this phase
# does is narrower -- make the applications that DO run use the GPU Windows is
# already handing through, and stop GTK complaining on every launch.

if should_run wslg; then
    phase "Phase: wslg"

    if [[ ! -d /mnt/wslg ]]; then
        msg "no /mnt/wslg -- no way to display a window here, skipping the GUI stack"
        msg "  (WSL1, or a Windows build without WSLg. Nothing else needs this.)"
    else
        export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

        # The Windows side of the bridge. Absent on WSL1 and on some older
        # builds; without it the Mesa d3d12 driver has nothing to talk to, so
        # say that plainly rather than installing drivers that cannot bind.
        if [[ -e /usr/lib/wsl/lib/libd3d12.so ]]; then
            msg "GPU passthrough present (/usr/lib/wsl/lib/libd3d12.so)"
        else
            warn "no /usr/lib/wsl/lib/libd3d12.so -- Windows is not passing a GPU through."
            warn "  Mesa still installs; rendering will be software (llvmpipe)."
        fi

        msg "Installing the GUI stack (${#WSLG_PKGS[@]} packages)..."
        apt_install "${WSLG_PKGS[@]}"

        # Installing Mesa is not enough, and this is the part that is not
        # guessable: WSLg's X server does not advertise DRI3
        # ("screen 0 does not appear to be DRI3 capable"), so Mesa's automatic
        # driver selection never reaches the d3d12 gallium driver and silently
        # settles on llvmpipe. Everything looks installed and everything renders
        # on the CPU. GALLIUM_DRIVER=d3d12 selects it explicitly.
        #
        # MESA_LOADER_DRIVER_OVERRIDE does NOT work here -- measured, not
        # assumed: it drives the DRI loader, while this needs the gallium
        # driver picked directly, and setting it leaves you on llvmpipe.
        #
        # Written to /etc/profile.d rather than to ~/.zshrc for two reasons: it
        # is a machine property rather than a user preference, and profile.d is
        # read by bash and zsh alike -- which matters because a Kali WSL image
        # defaults to bash, so a zshrc-only setting would reach nobody here.
        #
        # PROBED, never assumed. Forcing a gallium driver that cannot load
        # leaves GL broken outright rather than slow, which is worse than the
        # problem being solved. So the driver file and /dev/dxg must both exist
        # AND glxinfo must actually come back with a non-software renderer
        # before the setting is persisted.
        wslg_gpu_probe() {
            local out
            [[ -e /dev/dxg ]] || return 1
            [[ -e /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/dri/d3d12_dri.so ]] || return 1
            command -v glxinfo &>/dev/null || return 1
            out=$(sudo -u "$REAL_USER" env DISPLAY="${DISPLAY:-:0}" GALLIUM_DRIVER=d3d12 \
                  timeout 20 glxinfo -B 2>/dev/null \
                  | awk -F': ' '/OpenGL renderer string/ {print $2}')
            [[ -n "$out" && "$out" != *llvmpipe* && "$out" != *softpipe* ]] || return 1
            echo "$out"
        }

        if (( DRY_RUN )); then
            echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} probe d3d12, then write /etc/profile.d/wslg-gpu.sh"
        elif gpu_renderer=$(wslg_gpu_probe); then
            msg "d3d12 probe succeeded: $gpu_renderer"
            write_file /etc/profile.d/wslg-gpu.sh 644 <<'GPUENV'
# Written by kali-deploy-wsl.
#
# WSLg's X server does not advertise DRI3, so Mesa's automatic driver selection
# falls back to llvmpipe and every GUI application renders on the CPU while
# looking perfectly well installed. Selecting the gallium driver explicitly is
# what binds the GPU Windows passes through as /usr/lib/wsl/lib/libd3d12.so.
#
# Verified at deploy time by running glxinfo with this set and confirming the
# renderer came back as something other than llvmpipe. Delete this file to go
# back to software rendering.
export GALLIUM_DRIVER=d3d12
GPUENV
            msg "wrote /etc/profile.d/wslg-gpu.sh (read by bash and zsh alike)"
            msg "  takes effect in the NEXT login shell; export it by hand for this one"
        else
            warn "d3d12 did not come back usable -- leaving Mesa on its default."
            warn "  GUI apps will render on the CPU (llvmpipe). Causes worth checking:"
            warn "    /dev/dxg missing        -> the host is not exposing the GPU"
            warn "    d3d12_dri.so missing    -> libgl1-mesa-dri did not install"
            warn "    no display              -> run this from a shell that has one"
        fi

        # Kali ships /etc/dconf/profile/user declaring `system-db:local`, but
        # never compiles the db that line refers to, so /etc/dconf/db/local does
        # not exist and every GTK application opens with:
        #
        #   dconf-WARNING **: unable to open file '/etc/dconf/db/local' ...
        #   expect degraded performance
        #
        # Harmless, and alarming enough to send you diagnosing a display problem
        # you do not have. `dconf update` compiles local.d into the db; the
        # directory has to exist first, and an empty one is fine -- the point is
        # that the file the profile promises is actually there.
        if [[ -f /etc/dconf/profile/user ]] \
           && grep -q 'system-db:local' /etc/dconf/profile/user 2>/dev/null \
           && [[ ! -f /etc/dconf/db/local ]]; then
            msg "compiling the dconf system db Kali's profile references but never built"
            run mkdir -p /etc/dconf/db/local.d
            if command -v dconf &>/dev/null; then
                run dconf update || warn "dconf update failed (cosmetic; GTK warnings will persist)"
            else
                warn "dconf binary not present -- skipping (cosmetic only)"
            fi
        else
            msg "dconf system db already consistent"
        fi
    fi
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase: tmux
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if should_run tmux; then
    phase "Phase: tmux"

    backup_once "$USER_HOME/.tmux.conf"

    write_file "$USER_HOME/.tmux.conf" <<'TMUX'
# ~/.tmux.conf -- kali-deploy-wsl
# Prefix stays the default Ctrl-b so every tutorial you read matches.

# Quality of life
set -g mouse on                 # click panes, drag borders, scroll with the wheel
set -g history-limit 100000     # deep scrollback for long tool output
set -g base-index 1             # windows start at 1 (matches the number row)
setw -g pane-base-index 1
set -g renumber-windows on      # keep window numbers gapless when you close one
set -g allow-rename off         # don't let programs rename your windows
set -sg escape-time 10          # snappy Esc (helps vim inside tmux)
set -g focus-events on

# True color. Windows Terminal handles both of these correctly; the override is
# what stops colours washing out to 16 inside tmux.
set -g default-terminal "tmux-256color"
set -ga terminal-overrides ",*256col*:Tc"

# Intuitive splits: prefix + | (vertical), prefix + - (horizontal),
# both opening in the current pane's directory.
bind | split-window -h -c "#{pane_current_path}"
bind - split-window -v -c "#{pane_current_path}"
unbind '"'
unbind %

# Vim-style pane navigation: prefix + h/j/k/l
bind h select-pane -L
bind j select-pane -D
bind k select-pane -U
bind l select-pane -R

# Reload config: prefix + r
bind r source-file ~/.tmux.conf \; display "tmux config reloaded"

# --- Windows clipboard ---
# The other scripts in this repo can hand a selection to an X clipboard. There
# isn't one here, so copy-mode pipes through clip.exe and the selection lands on
# the WINDOWS clipboard -- which is the one you are actually going to paste from.
#
# clip.exe appends a CR and does not understand UTF-8 on some builds; piping
# through it directly is still the least-surprising option, and `tr -d '\r'` is
# on the paste side (see pbpaste in the shell phase), not here.
bind -T copy-mode-vi y send-keys -X copy-pipe-and-cancel "clip.exe"
bind -T copy-mode    Enter send-keys -X copy-pipe-and-cancel "clip.exe"
bind ] run "powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command Get-Clipboard 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\\r' | tmux load-buffer - && tmux paste-buffer"

# --- Status bar (amber-on-dark, echoing the old polybar palette) ---
set -g status-interval 5
set -g status-style bg=colour235,fg=colour136
set -g status-left "#[fg=colour136,bold] #S #[default]"
set -g status-left-length 30
set -g status-right "#[fg=colour108]#(whoami)@#h #[fg=colour136]%Y-%m-%d %H:%M "
set -g status-right-length 60
setw -g window-status-current-style fg=colour136,bg=colour238,bold
setw -g window-status-current-format " #I:#W "
setw -g window-status-format " #I:#W "
TMUX

    run chown "$REAL_USER:$REAL_USER" "$USER_HOME/.tmux.conf"
    msg "tmux config written to $USER_HOME/.tmux.conf"
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase: shell -- append to Kali's zsh, don't replace it
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if should_run shell; then
    phase "Phase: shell"

    # The host browser, as the default for anything that opens one.
    #
    # An alias only helps when you are the one typing. gh, git web--browse,
    # xdg-open, Python's webbrowser module and most CLI tools that open a page
    # read $BROWSER and exec it -- and $BROWSER has to be a real executable,
    # because a child process cannot call a zsh function. So this is a file on
    # PATH rather than a shell alias.
    #
    # Deliberately NOT gated on WSLg. msedge.exe is a Windows process: it needs
    # interop, not a Linux display, so this works on WSL1 and on a host with no
    # WSLg at all -- which is exactly when you want it most, since there is then
    # no way to draw a Linux browser.
    #
    # explorer.exe rather than `cmd.exe /c start`: cmd refuses a UNC working
    # directory and prints a warning before doing the job, and the script is
    # usually invoked from somewhere under /home. explorer.exe exits nonzero
    # even when it succeeds, hence the explicit exit 0 -- without it every tool
    # that checks $? decides the browser failed and prints its own error.
    write_file /usr/local/bin/wsl-browser 755 <<'WSLBROWSER'
#!/bin/sh
# wsl-browser -- open a URL or file in the WINDOWS default browser, from WSL.
# Written by kali-deploy-wsl. Referenced by $BROWSER in ~/.zshrc.

[ $# -eq 0 ] && { echo "usage: wsl-browser <url|file> ..." >&2; exit 1; }

for a in "$@"; do
    # A Windows process cannot follow /home/... -- it needs the
    # \\wsl.localhost\<distro>\... form. Only translate things that exist, so a
    # URL is passed through untouched.
    [ -e "$a" ] && a=$(wslpath -w "$a")
    explorer.exe "$a" >/dev/null 2>&1
done
exit 0
WSLBROWSER
    msg "installed /usr/local/bin/wsl-browser (host browser as \$BROWSER)"

    # `edge` as a real command on PATH, not a shell function.
    #
    # A function only exists inside an interactive zsh that sourced ~/.zshrc, so
    # it is unavailable to scripts, to `sudo edge`, to anything spawned by a
    # tool, and to bash. A file in /usr/local/bin is just a command, and behaves
    # like one everywhere.
    #
    # The install path is probed at RUN time, not baked in at deploy time: Edge
    # sits under "Program Files (x86)" or "Program Files" depending on channel
    # and age, and a value captured during a deploy goes stale on the first
    # major update -- on a machine where the deploy is not going to be re-run.
    write_file /usr/local/bin/edge 755 <<'EDGE'
#!/bin/sh
# edge -- launch Microsoft Edge on the Windows host, from inside WSL.
# Written by kali-deploy-wsl.
#
# This is a WINDOWS process. It does not see this instance's /etc/hosts, it is
# not affected by proxychains, and its trust store is Windows'. For anything
# going through Burp or ZAP, use the Linux firefox instead.

EXE=""
for c in "/mnt/c/Program Files (x86)/Microsoft/Edge/Application/msedge.exe" \
         "/mnt/c/Program Files/Microsoft/Edge/Application/msedge.exe"; do
    [ -x "$c" ] && { EXE="$c"; break; }
done

if [ -z "$EXE" ]; then
    echo "edge: msedge.exe not found under /mnt/c." >&2
    echo "      Is Edge installed on the host, and is /mnt/c mounted?" >&2
    exit 1
fi

# Translate only arguments that name an existing path; a URL or a --flag is
# passed through untouched. A Windows process cannot follow /home/...
for a in "$@"; do
    if [ -e "$a" ]; then set -- "$@" "$(wslpath -w "$a")"; else set -- "$@" "$a"; fi
    shift
done

# Detached, so closing the pane that launched it does not take it down. Edge
# normally hands off to an already-running session and returns immediately.
"$EXE" "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1 &
exit 0
EDGE
    msg "installed /usr/local/bin/edge"

    # Kali already ships a tuned zsh (syntax highlighting, autosuggestions, a
    # good prompt) and makes it the default shell. We append a marker-guarded
    # block rather than installing Oh My Zsh over the top: faster startup, and no
    # sed against a template that may not be there. Re-running is a no-op.
    #
    # No font probe here, unlike kali-deploy-physical. fc-list would be asking
    # the guest whether it can render a glyph that Windows Terminal draws, so the
    # answer would be about the wrong machine. Install a Nerd Font on Windows and
    # select it in the terminal profile; VINNY_ASCII=1 is the fallback.
    # Make zsh the login shell, because everything below is written for it.
    #
    # This is not a preference, it is the difference between the phase working
    # and the phase doing nothing. A Kali WSL rootfs defaults to /bin/bash --
    # unlike the ISO, which ships zsh as the default and is what the rest of
    # this repo was written against. On a bash login, ~/.zshrc is never sourced,
    # so the whole block below, the dots hook it guards, lfcd and the interop
    # aliases all sit on disk and reach nobody. The first real run on a WSL box
    # ended exactly that way: every phase reported success and the shell was
    # unchanged.
    #
    # SPEC.md already records this failure for kali-deploy-physical, where it
    # was the rare install path. Here it is the default one.
    #
    # chsh only rewrites /etc/passwd; it does not touch the running shell, so
    # the change lands on the next login -- a new terminal tab. Reversible with
    # `chsh -s /bin/bash`, and skippable with --keep-shell.
    if (( KEEP_SHELL )); then
        msg "--keep-shell: leaving the login shell alone"
        [[ "$(getent passwd "$REAL_USER" | cut -d: -f7)" != *zsh ]] \
            && warn "  note: login shell is not zsh, so the block below will not be sourced"
    else
        current_shell=$(getent passwd "$REAL_USER" | cut -d: -f7)
        target_shell=$(command -v zsh || echo /usr/bin/zsh)
        if [[ "$current_shell" == *zsh ]]; then
            msg "login shell is already $current_shell"
        elif [[ ! -x "$target_shell" ]]; then
            warn "zsh is not installed -- leaving the login shell as $current_shell"
            warn "  (the tools phase installs it; retry with --only shell afterwards)"
        else
            # chsh validates against /etc/shells and fails if zsh is missing
            # from it, which happens on a rootfs where zsh arrived after the
            # file was written.
            grep -qxF "$target_shell" /etc/shells 2>/dev/null \
                || { msg "adding $target_shell to /etc/shells"; \
                     run sh -c "echo '$target_shell' >> /etc/shells"; }
            msg "login shell is $current_shell; the config here is zsh -- switching"
            if run chsh -s "$target_shell" "$REAL_USER"; then
                msg "chsh: $REAL_USER -> $target_shell"
                warn "  takes effect in your NEXT shell -- open a new terminal tab"
            else
                warn "chsh failed -- leaving $current_shell (rerun with --only shell)"
            fi
        fi
    fi

    ZSHRC="$USER_HOME/.zshrc"

    if [[ ! -f "$ZSHRC" ]]; then
        warn "$ZSHRC does not exist -- creating one"
        run touch "$ZSHRC"
        run chown "$REAL_USER:$REAL_USER" "$ZSHRC"
    fi

    if grep -qF "$ZSHRC_MARKER" "$ZSHRC" 2>/dev/null; then
        msg "zshrc block already present (idempotent -- leaving it alone)"
    elif (( DRY_RUN )); then
        echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} append config block to $ZSHRC"
    else
        backup_once "$ZSHRC"
        cat >> "$ZSHRC" <<ZSHRC_BLOCK

$ZSHRC_MARKER
# Added by kali-deploy-wsl. Delete this block (through the closing
# marker) to remove. Kali's own zsh config above is untouched.

export PATH="\$HOME/.local/bin:\$HOME/.cargo/bin:\$PATH"

export ROCKYOU="/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt"

# Security
alias nse='ls /usr/share/nmap/scripts/ | grep'

# Proxychains
alias pchains='proxychains4'
alias pc='proxychains4'

# Network. No tsip alias here -- Tailscale runs on the Windows host, so the
# tailnet address belongs to the host and 'tailscale ip' has nothing to answer
# with in here.
alias extip='curl -s ifconfig.me'     # what the internet sees; \`myip\` is local
# The Windows host as WSL2 sees it, which is what you point a listener at when
# something on Windows needs to reach a service running in here.
alias winhost="ip route show default | awk '{print \\\$3}'"

# tmux
alias ta='tmux attach -t'             # ta main
alias tn='tmux new -s'                # tn main
alias tl='tmux ls'
alias tk='tmux kill-session -t'       # tk main

# --- Windows interop ---
# clip.exe comes from the Windows host and is always there. wslview comes from
# wslu, which Kali does not package and this script does not install, so the
# opener normally resolves to the explorer.exe branch -- probed at shell startup
# rather than at deploy time so it upgrades itself if you install wslu by hand.
# explorer.exe exits nonzero even when it succeeds, hence the '|| true' --
# without it every \`open\` looks like a failure.
alias pbcopy='clip.exe'
pbpaste() { powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command Get-Clipboard 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\\r'; }
if command -v wslview &>/dev/null; then
    alias open='wslview'
else
    open() { explorer.exe "\$@" || true; }
fi

# The browser session stays on the HOST by default.
#
# \$BROWSER is what gh, git web--browse, xdg-open and Python's webbrowser module
# exec when they want to show you a page, so pointing it at wsl-browser is what
# makes "open a browser from inside WSL" mean the Windows one everywhere --
# not just when you type \`open\` yourself. Both wsl-browser and edge are real
# files in /usr/local/bin, so scripts and non-zsh shells get them too.
#
#   edge <url|file>   Edge on the host, explicitly.
#   open <url|file>   the host's default handler, whatever that is.
#   firefox           the LINUX browser, and the deliberate exception below.
#
# Two jobs, and the reason to keep them apart is not speed. Edge is a Windows
# process: it cannot see this instance's /etc/hosts, proxychains does not apply
# to it, and its trust store is Windows'. So anything going through Burp or ZAP
# wants the Linux firefox instead -- that keeps the intercepting CA in a Linux
# profile rather than the Windows system store, resolves the vhost entries you
# added here, and keeps engagement traffic out of a browser signed into your
# personal account and syncing history to it.
export BROWSER=/usr/local/bin/wsl-browser
# Windows user profile, resolved at call time rather than baked in at deploy:
# the path contains the Windows username, which is not knowable from a root
# shell here (sudo has already scrubbed the environment that would carry it).
winhome() {
    local p
    p=\$(cmd.exe /c "echo %USERPROFILE%" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\\r')
    [[ -n "\$p" ]] && wslpath -u "\$p"
}
alias cdwin='cd "\$(winhome)"'

# Files. A child process cannot change its parent's directory, so lf prints
# where it ended up and the wrapper does the cd -- without this you always land
# back where you started, which is the one thing you notice missing. Named
# lfcd rather than aliased over lf so plain \`lf\` stays the real binary.
# Outside the dots guard below on purpose. That guard is for things dots does
# better, and it is allowed to be absent -- but THIS script installs lf, so its
# one usability wrapper must not hinge on a private repo whose phase is designed
# to skip.
lfcd() {
    local dir
    dir=\$(command lf -print-last-dir "\$@") && [[ -d "\$dir" ]] && cd "\$dir"
}

# Everything in this block duplicates something the dots shell kit provides, so
# define it only when dots is absent. The box ends up fully equipped either way,
# and nothing collides when dots is there.
#
# It has to be a guard rather than a deletion because the dots phase is allowed
# to fail -- and \`alias serve\` in particular would otherwise shadow dots'
# serve() permanently, since zsh resolves aliases before functions.
if [[ ! -r "\$HOME/dots/shell/zshrc" ]]; then
    export WORDLISTS="/usr/share/wordlists"
    export SECLISTS="/usr/share/seclists"
    alias serve='python3 -m http.server'
    alias ports='ss -tulanp'
    alias listening='ss -tlnp'
    alias myip="ip -4 -o addr show | awk '{print \\\$2, \\\$4}' | grep -v '^lo '"
    b64e() { echo -n "\$1" | base64; }
    b64d() { echo -n "\$1" | base64 -d; }
    urlencode() { python3 -c "import urllib.parse,sys; print(urllib.parse.quote(sys.argv[1]))" "\$1"; }
    urldecode() { python3 -c "import urllib.parse,sys; print(urllib.parse.unquote(sys.argv[1]))" "\$1"; }
fi

# tmux auto-attach is OPT-IN here, the reverse of kali-deploy-remote's default.
# There, a dropped SSH connection kills your work, so attaching every login to a
# persistent session is a straight win. Here you enter with wsl.exe and every
# Windows Terminal tab is a fresh login shell -- auto-attaching them all to one
# session means the second tab steals the first one's panes, which is worse than
# no tmux at all. Set AUTO_TMUX=1 if you want the remote behaviour.
if [[ -n "\${AUTO_TMUX:-}" && -z "\$TMUX" && \$- == *i* ]] \\
   && command -v tmux &>/dev/null; then
    tmux attach -t main 2>/dev/null || tmux new -s main
fi
$ZSHRC_END_MARKER
ZSHRC_BLOCK
        chown "$REAL_USER:$REAL_USER" "$ZSHRC"
        msg "appended config block to $ZSHRC"
    fi
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase: dots -- the personal shell kit, cloned and left to install itself
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Carried over unchanged from kali-deploy-remote, deliberately: the shell kit at
# github.com/owlshells/dots is the one thing every script in this repo deploys
# the same way, and a second implementation of the transport chain is a second
# thing to get wrong.
#
# On fonts: dots' prompt draws three Nerd Fonts private-use glyphs, and this
# script installs no font and does not probe for one. The glyphs are rendered by
# Windows Terminal, so the font situation inside the instance says nothing about
# what you will see. Install a patched font on Windows; if you get tofu, set
# VINNY_ASCII=1 and the prompt switches to plain characters.

if should_run dots; then
    phase "Phase: dots"

    DOTS_SLUG="${DOTS_REPO:-owlshells/dots}"
    DOTS_DIR="$USER_HOME/dots"

    # git must never block on a prompt. Without these, a host key it has not
    # seen or a private repo over HTTPS sits waiting for input that is not
    # coming.
    DOTS_SSH_OPTS="ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=10 -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new"

    as_user() {
        sudo -u "$REAL_USER" -H env GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 \
            GIT_SSH_COMMAND="$DOTS_SSH_OPTS" "$@"
    }

    # DOTS_REPO defaults to a public repo, but a box may still need to reach a
    # private fork, so try authenticated transports first -- most deliberate
    # first -- and fall back to a plain anonymous clone, which is all a public
    # repo ever needed. Treat total failure as a skip: a missing shell kit is an
    # inconvenience, not a reason to redo an install.
    dots_transport() {
        [[ -n "${DOTS_TOKEN:-}" ]] && { echo token; return 0; }
        as_user git ls-remote "git@github.com:${DOTS_SLUG}.git" &>/dev/null \
            && { echo ssh; return 0; }
        as_user gh auth token &>/dev/null && { echo gh; return 0; }
        as_user git ls-remote "https://github.com/${DOTS_SLUG}.git" &>/dev/null \
            && { echo public; return 0; }
        return 1
    }

    # A token goes in a per-invocation Authorization header, never in the URL.
    # A URL-embedded credential is persisted into .git/config by clone and gets
    # echoed back in git's own error messages -- and this script's transcript
    # lands in /var/log. This way the stored remote is the clean HTTPS one and
    # there is nothing to scrub afterwards.
    dots_clone() {
        local tok hdr
        case "$1" in
            ssh)
                as_user git clone --quiet "git@github.com:${DOTS_SLUG}.git" "$DOTS_DIR"
                ;;
            token|gh)
                if [[ "$1" == token ]]; then tok="$DOTS_TOKEN"; else tok=$(as_user gh auth token); fi
                hdr=$(printf 'x-access-token:%s' "$tok" | base64 -w0)
                as_user git -c "http.extraHeader=Authorization: Basic $hdr" \
                    clone --quiet "https://github.com/${DOTS_SLUG}.git" "$DOTS_DIR"
                ;;
            public)
                as_user git clone --quiet "https://github.com/${DOTS_SLUG}.git" "$DOTS_DIR"
                ;;
        esac
    }

    dots_run_install() {
        if [[ ! -x "$DOTS_DIR/install.sh" ]]; then
            warn "no install.sh in $DOTS_DIR -- clone looks wrong, leaving it"
            return 1
        fi
        chown -R "$REAL_USER:$REAL_USER" "$DOTS_DIR" 2>/dev/null
        # --no-tmux: this box's ~/.tmux.conf is the one written by the tmux
        # phase above, with the clip.exe bindings the repo's config has no
        # reason to carry. It also stops write_file's `cat >` landing inside the
        # dots checkout.
        if as_user "$DOTS_DIR/install.sh" --no-tmux >/dev/null; then
            msg "dots installed (~/.bash_aliases, ~/.zshrc hook)"
        else
            warn "dots install.sh reported a problem -- shell kit may be partial"
            return 1
        fi
        # Give recall something to work with on the very first shell.
        [[ -x "$DOTS_DIR/tools/backfill-index.sh" ]] \
            && as_user "$DOTS_DIR/tools/backfill-index.sh" >/dev/null 2>&1
        return 0
    }

    if (( DRY_RUN )); then
        echo -e "${YELLOW}[dry-run]${NC} clone/update ${DOTS_SLUG} into ${DOTS_DIR}, then run its install.sh"
    elif ! command -v git &>/dev/null; then
        # Only reachable via --only dots on a bare box; the base phase installs
        # git long before this normally runs. Still worth a way forward.
        warn "git is not installed -- skipping dots"
        warn "  install it and retry: sudo $0 --only dots"
    elif [[ -d "$DOTS_DIR/.git" ]]; then
        msg "dots already present -- updating"
        as_user git -C "$DOTS_DIR" pull --ff-only &>/dev/null \
            && msg "dots updated" \
            || warn "could not fast-forward $DOTS_DIR (local changes?) -- installing what is there"
        dots_run_install
    elif [[ -e "$DOTS_DIR" ]]; then
        warn "$DOTS_DIR exists but is not a git checkout -- leaving it alone"
    else
        DOTS_TR=$(dots_transport) || DOTS_TR=""
        if [[ -z "$DOTS_TR" ]]; then
            warn "could not reach ${DOTS_SLUG} (tried token/ssh/gh auth, and an"
            warn "  anonymous clone) -- skipping. If it's a private fork, finish"
            warn "  it later with:"
            warn "    sudo DOTS_TOKEN=<token> $0 --only dots"
            warn "  (or restore an SSH key, or install gh and \`gh auth login\` --"
            warn "   gh ships from GitHub's apt repo, not Kali's, so it is not here)"
        elif dots_clone "$DOTS_TR"; then
            msg "cloned ${DOTS_SLUG} over ${DOTS_TR}"
            dots_run_install
        else
            warn "clone of ${DOTS_SLUG} over ${DOTS_TR} failed -- skipping (run unaffected)"
        fi
    fi
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase: wordlists
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

if should_run wordlists; then
    phase "Phase: wordlists"

    # Kali ships rockyou gzipped; decompress in place, keeping the .gz.
    if [[ -f "$WORDLIST_DIR/rockyou.txt" ]]; then
        msg "rockyou.txt already present"
    elif [[ -f "$WORDLIST_DIR/rockyou.txt.gz" ]]; then
        run gunzip -k "$WORDLIST_DIR/rockyou.txt.gz" && msg "decompressed rockyou.txt"
    else
        warn "rockyou.txt(.gz) not found (is the 'wordlists' package installed?)"
    fi

    if [[ -d /usr/share/seclists ]]; then
        msg "SecLists present at /usr/share/seclists"
    else
        warn "SecLists not found -- 'apt install seclists'"
    fi
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verification
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

phase "Verification"

if (( DRY_RUN )); then
    msg "Dry run complete -- nothing was changed."
    exit 0
fi

check() {
    if command -v "$1" &>/dev/null; then
        echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} $1"
    else
        echo -e "${RED}x${NC} $1"
    fi
}

# For things whose absence is expected and handled, so red keeps meaning
# "something is wrong". wslview is the case this exists for: Kali has no wslu
# package, so a plain check would print an X on every correct run.
check_opt() {
    local what="$1" note="${2:-optional}"
    if command -v "$what" &>/dev/null; then
        echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} $what"
    else
        echo -e "${YELLOW}-${NC} $what ($note)"
    fi
}

echo ""
echo "Security tools:"
for c in "${VERIFY_BINS_TOOLS[@]}"; do
    check "$c"
done

echo ""
echo "Shell environment:"
for c in "${VERIFY_BINS_SHELL[@]}"; do
    check "$c"
done
[[ -r "$USER_HOME/dots/shell/zshrc" ]] \
    && echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} dots (${DOTS_REPO:-owlshells/dots})" \
    || echo -e "${RED}x${NC} dots (${DOTS_REPO:-owlshells/dots}) -- rerun: sudo $0 --only dots"

# The check that would have caught the first run's silent no-op. Everything
# above can be green while the shell that reads none of it is the one you get
# at login, so the login shell is the tick that makes the others mean anything.
login_shell=$(getent passwd "$REAL_USER" | cut -d: -f7)
if [[ "$login_shell" == *zsh ]]; then
    echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} login shell: $login_shell"
else
    echo -e "${RED}x${NC} login shell: $login_shell -- ~/.zshrc is NOT sourced,"
    echo -e "    so the block this script wrote reaches nothing. Fix with:"
    echo -e "      chsh -s $(command -v zsh || echo /usr/bin/zsh) $REAL_USER"
fi

echo ""
echo "Windows interop:"
# These have to be asked in the right context, or they answer a different
# question than the one that matters. The first real run got four red Xs here
# on a box where all four were fine.
#
# clip.exe and powershell.exe come from the Windows PATH that interop appends to
# the USER's environment. sudo resets PATH, so checking them from this root
# shell tests whether root can see them -- which nothing depends on, and which
# is false on a perfectly good box. Ask as the user who will actually type them.
check_user() {
    if sudo -u "$REAL_USER" -H sh -lc "command -v $1 >/dev/null 2>&1"; then
        echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} $1"
    else
        echo -e "${RED}x${NC} $1 (not on ${REAL_USER}'s PATH)"
    fi
}

# For files this script wrote, "is it on PATH" is the wrong question entirely --
# we know exactly where we put it, so check that, and a stale PATH cannot make
# a successful install look like a failure.
check_file() {
    if [[ -x "$1" ]]; then
        echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} $(basename "$1")"
    else
        echo -e "${RED}x${NC} $(basename "$1") (expected at $1)"
    fi
}

check_user clip.exe
check_user powershell.exe
check_file /usr/local/bin/edge
check_file /usr/local/bin/wsl-browser
check_opt wslview "not packaged for Kali -- 'open' falls back to explorer.exe"
[[ -d /mnt/c ]] && echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} /mnt/c mounted" \
                || echo -e "${RED}x${NC} /mnt/c not mounted"
if [[ -f "$WSLCONF" ]] && grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*options[[:space:]]*=.*metadata' "$WSLCONF"; then
    echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} automount metadata configured"
else
    echo -e "${RED}x${NC} automount metadata not configured (chmod under /mnt/c will not stick)"
fi
has_systemd && echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} systemd is PID 1" \
            || echo -e "${RED}x${NC} systemd is not PID 1 -- 'wsl --shutdown' from Windows"

if [[ -d /mnt/wslg ]]; then
    echo ""
    echo "GUI (WSLg):"
    check firefox     # the binary is firefox; the package is firefox-esr
    check glxinfo
    # The claim worth checking is not "Mesa is installed" but "the GPU is the
    # thing rendering". glxinfo names the renderer, so read it rather than
    # inferring it from a package list: d3d12 means the bridge to the Windows
    # GPU bound, llvmpipe means Mesa fell back to the CPU and every window will
    # feel like it.
    # Ask as the user, with a display, and with the environment a login shell
    # will actually have -- i.e. including whatever /etc/profile.d/wslg-gpu.sh
    # sets. Running this bare from the root shell reports llvmpipe on a box
    # where GUI apps are about to render on the GPU perfectly well, which is
    # what the first real run did.
    if command -v glxinfo &>/dev/null; then
        gl_renderer=$(sudo -u "$REAL_USER" -H env DISPLAY="${DISPLAY:-:0}" \
                      sh -lc 'timeout 20 glxinfo -B 2>/dev/null' \
                      | awk -F': ' '/OpenGL renderer string/ {print $2}')
        if [[ -z "$gl_renderer" ]]; then
            echo -e "${RED}x${NC} GL renderer -- glxinfo returned nothing (is WSLg running?)"
        elif [[ "$gl_renderer" == *llvmpipe* || "$gl_renderer" == *softpipe* ]]; then
            echo -e "${YELLOW}-${NC} GL renderer: $gl_renderer (software)"
            echo -e "    /etc/profile.d/wslg-gpu.sh is read at LOGIN, so this is expected"
            echo -e "    on the run that created it. Open a new shell and re-check with:"
            echo -e "      glxinfo -B | grep renderer"
        else
            echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} GL renderer: $gl_renderer"
        fi
    fi
    [[ -f /etc/dconf/db/local ]] \
        && echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} dconf system db (GTK apps start quietly)" \
        || echo -e "${YELLOW}-${NC} dconf system db absent (harmless GTK warnings on launch)"
fi

echo ""
echo "Wordlists:"
[[ -f "$WORDLIST_DIR/rockyou.txt" ]] \
    && echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} rockyou.txt" || echo -e "${RED}x${NC} rockyou.txt"
[[ -d "${SECLISTS:-/usr/share/seclists}" || -d "$WORDLIST_DIR/seclists" ]] \
    && echo -e "${GREEN}/${NC} SecLists"   || echo -e "${RED}x${NC} SecLists"

cat <<BANNER

==============================================
  Deploy complete (Kali on WSL)
==============================================

  Log: $LOGFILE

  Open a new shell to pick up the zsh block, or:
    exec zsh

  Windows interop:
    somecmd | pbcopy     put output on the Windows clipboard
    pbpaste              read the Windows clipboard
    cdwin                cd to your Windows user profile
    winhost              the host's IP as seen from in here

  Browsing (the session stays on the host):
    edge <url|file>      Edge on Windows; \$BROWSER routes gh/xdg-open here too
    open <url|file>      the host's default handler
    firefox <url>        the LINUX browser -- use this one through Burp/ZAP, so
                         the CA stays out of the Windows store and /etc/hosts
                         entries actually resolve

  tmux (not auto-attached here -- every terminal tab is its own login):
    tn main                start a session      ta main   re-attach
    Ctrl-b c   new window      Ctrl-b n/p  next/prev window
    Ctrl-b |   split vert      Ctrl-b -    split horiz
    Ctrl-b [   copy mode, then y copies to the WINDOWS clipboard
    Ctrl-b ]   paste from the Windows clipboard
  Set AUTO_TMUX=1 in your environment to attach on every login instead.

BANNER

if ! has_systemd; then
    cat <<'SYSTEMD_NOTE'
  NOTE: systemd is not PID 1 yet. From a Windows terminal:
      wsl --shutdown
  then start Kali again. Nothing in this script needs re-running.

SYSTEMD_NOTE
fi

# What is only observable by using the box, so the script says it rather than
# ticking it. Same idea as kali-deploy-vm's closing checklist: a verification
# line that cannot actually fail teaches you to skim past the ones that can.
cat <<'UNVERIFIED'
  Not verified by this script:
    - Whether Windows Terminal is using a Nerd Font. The glyphs in the dots
      prompt are drawn by Windows, so nothing in here can check. If you see
      boxes, install a patched font on Windows and select it in the profile,
      or set VINNY_ASCII=1.
    - Whether /etc/wsl.conf changes have taken effect. They need a
      'wsl --shutdown' from the host, which this script cannot trigger.
    - Whether GUI tools display. WSLg being present means they should; no
      window manager is installed and none is wanted (see the header).

UNVERIFIED
